{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b72e-dc34-7508-89d4-a5d4024f9fea","session_id":"01a1b72e-43b0-76b3-955b-d3f444f96522","message":{"id":"01a1b72edc34750889d4a5d2e8f603dd","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So AC-006 becomes covered iff T-003.json declares it covered. No way to have it planned while also covered=10.\n\nHmm, wait. The test asserts `merged.acs['AC-006'] === 'planned'`. What if T-003.json declares AC-006 with status 'planned' and there's another shard... no, only one shard per AC allowed (shard.duplicate). And V-R-2 requires 10 covered / 64 planned, i.e., exactly 4 new covered: AC-006,007,008,072 all covered.\n\nSo this is genuinely a deadlock, given the constraints. The prior dev concluded the same and left it. Four rounds of review confirm.\n\nNow, I'm dev round 2 (per live.json) — but maybe the orchestrator restarted with a new design? Let me check whether design.md/acceptance.md were modified relative to HEAD commit... mtime 01:35 vs review at 02:03. So no.\n\nHmm, but wait: is it possible the design intends `test/unit/traceability.test.mjs` to be under `test/unit/**`? No, write_scope lists explicit files.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe there's an in-scope legit change that makes this pass. What determines whether check-fast runs `test/unit/traceability.test.mjs`? `npm test` → scripts/run-tests.mjs --full. Discovery includes all test/**/*.test.mjs. Not in scope.\n\nBUT: does the test import `../../scripts/check-traceability.mjs`? Yes. scripts/check-traceability.mjs is NOT in write_scope either.\n\nWhat if the AC-006 test in T-003.json... Consider `merged.acs['AC-006']`: it's from `declared`. Could there be a way that T-003.json exists as a valid shard for coverage in check-traceability but `mergeShards` with `root` = ROOT... same thing. Same function, same files.\n\nUnless... check-traceability's `--json` probe in V-R-2 uses spawnSync with default cwd, root = cwd. And the test also uses ROOT. Same.\n\nSo no.\n\nTherefore, in-scope resolution is impossible. What should I, as dev, do? The user's instructions are strict: only modify write_scope files, make every acceptance command pass.\n\nOptions:\nA. Report the deadlock again in impl.md, change nothing (like round 4). That yields another needs_revision loop.\nB. Fix test/unit/traceability.test.mjs (out of scope) → V-R-5 fails and violates explicit instruction.\nC. Find a way that is arguably in-scope and legitimate.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider option C more creatively. What about `test/unit/traceability.test.mjs` — is a *file permission/read* trick possible? E.g., check-fast runs npm test... no.\n\nWhat about the possibility that `test/traceability/T-003.json` is not the only shard; maybe if AC-006's status in T-003.json were 'planned', and the four ACs got covered from... hmm, covered=10 requires AC-006 covered.\n\nAlternatively: does the AC-004 test use `readIndex()` = contracts/spec/ac-index.json, and mergeShards({root: ROOT, index})? If AC-006 were NOT in the index (indexAcs), then `acs['AC-006']` would be undefined, and assert.equal(undefined, 'planned') fails too. And shard entry would error unknown_ac.\n\nSo truly deadlocked.\n\nHmm, unless... `declared` requires `shard.task === owners.get(ac)`? No, mismatch only pushes error; declared.set still happens (before the owner check). Yes declared.set happens regardless.\n\nOK. So the honest answer: deadlock. But is the orchestrator aware? notice.json says blocked/review_exhausted. The orchestrator relaunched dev again (round 2 in live.json — weird, maybe a fresh counter for a new phase?).\n\nLet me read notice.json and run.json / state.json to understand what the orchestrator expects of me now. run.json is 10KB. state.json 2.5KB. Reading them is allowed (only modification forbidden).","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_to