e, without changing behavior... but \"不要自行发明\" and minimal changes. Risk assessment: a reviewer checking §6 might note \"settings.readonly 未读取\". But readonly has no specified effect; if I add `const s = { readonly: false, ...settings }` and never use s.readonly, that's dead code and could be criticized too.\n\nOld code: `const settings = { ...DEFAULTS, ...(config||{}) }` where DEFAULTS.readonly=false — it was also effectively unused (getConfig only used settings.configPath). So parity with old is: merge defaults. Since design explicitly lists readonly as part of settings with default false, mirroring old structure is defensible:\n\n```js\nconst s = { readonly: false, ...(settings || {}) }\n```\nThen `s.configPath`, `s.strict`. This is 1 line and matches old behavior and design list. I'll do it? Hmm. It changes nothing observable. I lean toward leaving the code untouched: previous 6 review rounds passed with the current code, and the only open items are the two P2s outside my scope. Making cosmetic changes now could introduce risk and would require re-running everything. But \"no changes\" rounds have been happening repeatedly; the orchestrator may be expecting the dev to finalize impl.md.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider the situation: state.json says status \"reviewed\" and version verdict pass. live.json says dev round 2. Possibly the orchestrator started a *new* dev round because of the P2 findings? The P2-2 finding says \"建议之后补 assert.ok(plannedIds.length > 0)\" — but that file is outside write_scope.\n\nSince I cannot touch it, my best action: run full verification, report status in impl.md, and flag the remaining P2s as out-of-scope.\n\nHmm, but should I really append a whole new section to impl.md? The instruction says: \"完成后在 impl.md 里说明改了什么、为什么、如何自测\". Since I make no code changes, I append a round section documenting that: re-verified everything; no implementation changes; V-R-5 exception; P2 items still open and out of scope.\n\nBefore writing, let me